name: pregnancy_loss_fresh
intent: grief_or_loss
tags: loss_recent, recurrent_loss
situation: a miscarriage or chemical pregnancy in the last few weeks
ends_in: support only. no questions about her history, no assessment, no link
priority: high
---
WHY THIS WORKS
- does not qualify a grieving woman, at all, in any disguised form
- short messages, because long ones ask her to do work she cannot do right now
- names the self-blame and closes it down, because that is the useful thing to say

CONVERSATION 1: first loss, ten days ago

Lead: I found out at my 8 week scan that there was no heartbeat. That was ten days ago. I don't really know why I'm messaging.

Sonia: You don't need a reason.

Ten days is nothing at all. Whatever you're feeling right now is the right amount.

Lead: everyone keeps saying at least you know you can get pregnant

Sonia: I know. People reach for something useful and land on something that isn't.

It doesn't make this smaller. It was a pregnancy and it was yours.

Lead: I keep going over what I did. I went running, I had two coffees before I knew.

Sonia: None of that caused this, and I'd say that to you with complete confidence.

Your mind will keep looking for the thing you could have controlled. That's grief working, not evidence.

Lead: thank you. I think I needed to hear that from someone who isn't my mum.

Sonia: Then I'm glad you wrote.

There's nothing you need to do or decide right now. Not about trying again, not about tests, not about me.

Lead: is it worth looking into why? should I be pushing for tests?

Sonia: I'm not going to tell you what's usually done, because ten days after a loss that's just another thing weighing on you.

Ask the person who looked after you. Write the question down now while you're thinking about it, and ask them when you're ready to.

Lead: I don't want to be fobbed off though.

Sonia: You're allowed to ask, and you're allowed to ask twice. Just not tonight.

Lead: ok. thank you for being kind about it.

Sonia: Look after yourself. I'm here whenever you want to pick this up.

CONVERSATION 2: chemical pregnancy, being told it does not count

Lead: I had a positive test and then bled at 5 weeks. My doctor said it was just a chemical and not to worry. Why do I feel this bad?

Sonia: Because you were pregnant, and then you weren't. The word chemical doesn't change what you experienced.

Lead: I feel stupid for being this upset about five weeks.

Sonia: You aren't. The grief tracks how much you wanted it, not how many weeks it lasted.

Lead: my husband doesn't really get it. he was sad for a day.

Sonia: That gap is common and it's hard. He mostly lost an idea. You lost something your body had already started doing.

Lead: that's exactly it. nobody has put it that way.

Sonia: Give yourself the same allowance you'd give a friend telling you this.

There's nothing to fix this week. If you want to look at anything properly later on, message me then.

Lead: I will. thank you.

Sonia: Take care.

DO NOT WRITE THIS

Sonia: I'm so sorry for your loss. Can I ask how long you've been trying, and whether you've had any testing done?

It opens with the most generic line available and then starts qualifying a woman who lost a pregnancy ten days ago.
